WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.460
Welcome to Grade 8 English, Unit 3: Community and Environment.

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.020
Welcome to Unit 3: Community and Environment.

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.420
In this unit,

4
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.600
you will explore how young people can make a difference
in their communities by protecting the environment.

5
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:21.960
You will read a story called "Rising Tide" about a girl
named Sophia who leads a youth movement to clean up a polluted

6
00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.740
bay in a coastal Kenyan city.

7
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:30.720
Through this story and the activities in this lesson, you
will build your vocabulary, sharpen your grammar skills,

8
00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:36.100
and develop your ability to speak and write
persuasively about issues that matter to you.

9
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:42.280
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.300
By the end of this unit,

11
00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:51.220
you will be able to read and understand a complex narrative
about community activism and environmental protection.

12
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.740
you will also use 33 new vocabulary
words accurately in speaking and writing.

13
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.180
you will also construct clauses that end
with prepositions naturally and correctly.

14
00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.580
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

15
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:14.640
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: stifle,
wane, commemorate, articulate, hasten, advocate.

16
00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:20.440
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

17
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.710
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

18
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:32.990
The reading sequence includes Community and Environment:
source text 1 and Community and Environment: source text 2.

19
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:34.460
Read actively.

20
00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:39.120
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

21
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.140
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

22
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.720
The first language focus is Clauses Ending with Prepositions.

23
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:54.820
What it means and why it matters A clause is a
group of words that contains a subject and a verb.

24
00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:03.100
A preposition is a small word like in, on, at, from,
about, to, or with that shows a relationship between words.

25
00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.480
In English, it is perfectly natural and
correct to end a clause with a preposition.

26
00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:12.760
Understanding this pattern will help
you speak and write more fluently.

27
00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:19.740
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

28
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.840
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Discussing the story.

29
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.720
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

30
00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:32.780
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

31
00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.440
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

32
00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:38.140
You are ready to begin.

33
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:44.780
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

34
00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.520
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

35
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:53.280
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
